.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Vt dtfl_config
says how many workers a computation runs and how they communicate.
It combines timely's communication configuration and its worker
configuration.
.Fn dtfl_config_new
returns a configuration for one worker thread, demand-driven progress,
no connection reports, seed 0 and no log addresses.
.Fn dtfl_config_free
releases it;
.Dv NULL
is ignored.
.Pp
The communication mode is one of:
.Bl -tag -width DTFL_COMM_PROCESS_BINARY
.It Dv DTFL_COMM_THREAD
One worker.
.It Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS
.Fa threads
workers in this process, which exchange messages through typed queues.
.It Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS_BINARY
.Fa threads
workers in this process, which exchange serialized messages through
zero-copy byte queues.
.It Dv DTFL_COMM_CLUSTER
.Fa threads
workers in each of several processes, connected over TCP.
.It Dv DTFL_COMM_SIM
.Fa threads
workers on one OS thread.
A scheduler seeded with
.Fa seed
picks the order in which messages are delivered, so a run can be
replayed exactly.
.El
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_config_set_workers
says where
.Fn dtfl_execute
runs the workers of a
.Dv DTFL_COMM_THREAD ,
.Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS
or
.Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS_BINARY
configuration:
.Bl -tag -width DTFL_WORKERS_PROCESSES
.It Dv DTFL_WORKERS_THREADS
Threads of the calling process, the default.
.It Dv DTFL_WORKERS_PROCESSES
One child process per worker.
.Fn dtfl_execute
makes a
.Xr socketpair 2
between every pair of workers, forks the children, and runs each worker
in its child with
.Xr dtfl_execute_worker 3
over
.Xr dtfl_transport_socket 3 ,
so no child starts a thread.
The workers share no memory: the context passed to
.Fn dtfl_execute
is each child's copy, and results go back to the parent only through
something the host sets up, such as a pipe or shared memory.
.Fn dtfl_execute
returns once every child has exited, 0 if all exited with status 0;
.Fa *guards
is set to
.Dv NULL .
.El
.Pp
With
.Dv DTFL_WORKERS_PROCESSES ,
.Fn dtfl_execute
fails with
.Dv DTFL_EINVAL
for the cluster and simulation modes, with
.Dv DTFL_ENOTSUP
on a platform without
.Xr fork 2 ,
and with
.Dv DTFL_EIO
if a child fails, exits non-zero or is killed.
Use
.Xr dtfl_execute_worker 3
directly for processes the host creates itself.
.Pp
Forked workers have the limits of any
.Xr fork 2
without
.Xr exec 3 :
.Bl -bullet
.It
In a parent that already runs other threads, a child starts with only
the forking thread, and a lock another thread held at the fork stays
held for ever.
The child allocates through the allocation hooks, so
.Xr malloc 3
or the host's allocator must be safe to use after a fork in a
multithreaded process (glibc's is); call
.Fn dtfl_execute
before starting threads where that is not so.
.It
A child ends with
.Xr _exit 2 ,
not
.Xr exit 3 :
.Xr atexit 3
handlers do not run and
.Xr stdio 3
buffers are not flushed.
Output a worker writes with
.Xr printf 3
to a pipe or a file is lost unless the worker calls
.Xr fflush 3 ;
anything buffered in the parent before the call is copied into every
child and dropped there, not written twice.
.It
The parent learns a child's result from
.Xr waitpid 2 .
If the caller has set
.Dv SIGCHLD
to
.Dv SIG_IGN ,
the kernel reaps the children and
.Xr waitpid 2
fails with
.Er ECHILD ;
each child then also reports success or failure on a pipe, so
.Fn dtfl_execute
still returns 0 for a good run, but a failure detail cannot name the
exit status or the signal.
.It
The mesh sockets and status pipes are close-on-exec
.Pf ( Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
where available), so a program another thread of the parent runs with
.Xr exec 3
meanwhile does not hold a worker's stream open.
.El
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_config_set_mode
and
.Fn dtfl_config_set_threads
set the mode and the number of worker threads per process.
.Fn dtfl_config_set_cluster
sets this process's index and every process's listening address,
written as
.Dq host:port .
An entry may list several whitespace-separated addresses to listen on.
It copies the strings and does not change the mode.
.Fn dtfl_config_set_report
asks for connection progress to be printed while a cluster starts.
.Fn dtfl_config_set_progress_mode
chooses between
.Dv DTFL_PROGRESS_DEMAND ,
the default, which holds back progress updates until they could
advance some frontier, and
.Dv DTFL_PROGRESS_EAGER ,
which sends them at once.
.Fn dtfl_config_set_seed
seeds the simulation scheduler.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_config_set_spill_file
lets the zero-copy byte queues spill to disk, so that a slow receiver
does not make the process hold every byte sent to it.
It applies to the queues between workers under
.Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS_BINARY
and to the queues between workers and the network threads under
.Dv DTFL_COMM_CLUSTER ;
the other modes have no byte queues and ignore it.
Each queue gets its own pair of policies.
The sending side counts the bytes resident in the queue.
When they exceed
.Fa head_reserve_bytes
+
.Fa threshold_bytes ,
it writes every queued buffer that starts after the first
.Fa head_reserve_bytes ,
except the newest one, to a new temporary file in
.Fa dir .
The receiving side reads spilled buffers back in order, about
.Fa head_reserve_bytes
at a time, just before it consumes them.
A buffer holds whole serialized messages, up to about a megabyte each,
so the bounds are approximate.
Each file is created with
.Xr mkstemp 3
and unlinked at once, so nothing is left in
.Fa dir
even if the process dies; it is closed when its last buffer has been
read back or the queue is dropped.
Message order and content are unchanged.
The directory name is copied.
Passing
.Dv NULL
for
.Fa dir
removes the hook; queues then never spill, which is the default.
.Pp
The functions without
.Dq set
in their names return the corresponding values.
.Fn dtfl_config_processes
is 1 unless cluster addresses are set.
.Fn dtfl_config_address
returns
.Dv NULL
when
.Fa i
is out of range.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_config_from_args
parses timely's command-line flags:
.Bl -tag -width "--progress-mode MODE"
.It Fl w , Fl \-threads Ar NUM
Worker threads per process (default 1).
.It Fl p , Fl \-process Ar IDX
This process's index (default 0).
.It Fl n , Fl \-processes Ar NUM
The number of processes (default 1).
.It Fl h , Fl \-hostfile Ar FILE
A file whose first
.Ar NUM
lines are the process addresses.
Without it, process
.Ar i
listens on
.Dq localhost:2101+i .
.It Fl r , Fl \-report
Report connection progress.
.It Fl z , Fl \-zerocopy
Use zero-copy channels within a process.
.It Fl \-progress-mode Ar MODE
.Dq eager
or
.Dq demand .
.El
.Pp
As with Rust's getopts, an argument can be attached
.Pq Fl w4
or separate
.Pq Fl w Ar 4 ,
long options accept
.Fl \-threads Ns = Ns Ar 4 ,
flags can be grouped
.Pq Fl rz ,
and giving an option twice is an error.
The mode follows timely: more than one process gives
.Dv DTFL_COMM_CLUSTER ;
otherwise more than one thread gives
.Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS ,
or
.Dv DTFL_COMM_PROCESS_BINARY
with
.Fl z ;
otherwise the mode is
.Dv DTFL_COMM_THREAD .
Parsing replaces the whole communication configuration.
The seed, the log addresses and the spill hook are not set on the
command line, so they are kept.
.Pp
Every argument that is not a timely flag is copied, in order, to
.Fa rest ,
and their count is stored in
.Fa *nrest .
That includes
.Fa argv[0] ,
free arguments, options timely does not recognize, and everything
after
.Dq \-\-
(which is itself dropped).
.Fa rest
must have room for
.Fa argc
pointers, and may be
.Fa argv
itself.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_config_from_env
applies the opt-in logging settings.
It calls
.Fa lookup
with the names
.Ev TIMELY_WORKER_LOG_ADDR
and
.Ev DIFFERENTIAL_LOG_ADDR ,
and copies each value that is not
.Dv NULL .
Pass a wrapper around
.Xr getenv 3
to use the process environment.
.Fn dtfl_config_timely_log_addr
and
.Fn dtfl_config_differential_log_addr
return the copies, or
.Dv NULL .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_config_new
never returns
.Dv NULL ;
allocation failure calls the panic hook.
The functions returning
.Vt int
return 0 on success, or an error code described below.
On error
.Fn dtfl_config_from_args
leaves
.Fa cfg ,
.Fa rest
and
.Fa *nrest
unchanged.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static const char *
env(const char *name, void *ctx)
{
	return getenv(name);
}

int
main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
	dtfl_config *cfg = dtfl_config_new();
	int nrest;

	if (dtfl_config_from_args(cfg, argc, argv, argv, &nrest) != 0 ||
	    dtfl_config_from_env(cfg, env, NULL) != 0)
		errx(1, "%s", dtfl_last_error_detail());
	/* argv[1 .. nrest) are the program's own arguments. */
	...
	dtfl_config_free(cfg);
}
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width DTFL_EINVAL
.It Bq Er DTFL_EINVAL
An argument is invalid: zero threads, an unknown mode, a process index
outside the cluster, a malformed or repeated flag, an unknown progress
mode, a host file with fewer lines than
.Fl n ,
or an empty spill directory or a zero
.Fa head_reserve_bytes .
.It Bq Er DTFL_EIO
The host file cannot be read, or
.Fn dtfl_config_set_spill_file
cannot create a file in
.Fa dir .
.El
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_last_error_detail
describes the problem.
.Pp
If a spill file cannot be written while the computation runs, the
buffers stay in memory and a warning goes to the log hook
.Pq Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 .
If a spilled buffer cannot be read back, the panic hook is called.
